## S4b. Ladder epochs: which record gaps can a WORD produce?
  Lemma (PROVED): if x_j > x_i and x_m < x_i for i < m < j (a first passage above x_i at lag g = j - i),
  and no pair in the window is a crossing, then 2^(K_m) > 3^m for 0 < m < g and 2^(K_g) < 3^g with the last
  exponent 1, which forces floor(g log2 3) - floor((g-1) log2 3) = 2 (g >= 2).  Excluded lags g <= 128
  (density 2 - log2 3 = 0.415): [3, 5, 8, 10, 13, 15, 17, 20, 22, 25, 27, 29, 32, 34, 37, 39, 41, 44, 46, 49, 51, 54, 56, 58, 61, 63, 66, 68, 70, 73, 75, 78, 80, 82, 85, 87, 90, 92, 94, 97, 99, 102, 104, 107, 109, 111, 114, 116, 119, 121, 123, 126, 128]
  (cross-checked for g <= 64 by a dynamic program over exponent words: identical)
  plus (10^7): max-record gaps realized: 61 values up to 118; EXCLUDED gaps realized: {17: (8, 171), 29: (57, 327), 41: (6, 1287), 46: (3, 2035)}; first-record indices realized at excluded lags: [17, 29, 41, 46]
  minus (10^7): max-record gaps realized: 63 values up to 112; EXCLUDED gaps realized: {}; first-record indices realized at excluded lags: []
  minus sheet, PROVED (computer-assisted): an excluded gap g needs a crossing (x_i, m), m < g, whose start
  x_i is a running maximum; on the minus sheet the crossings with m <= 35 are exactly (165|309|549, 12)
  (S4), and a running maximum x_i forces the start n <= x_i <= 549, inside the census.  Hence NO minus
  orbit (any n) has a max-record gap or first-record index g <= 36 that is excluded; to 10^7 no excluded
  gap of any size occurs on the minus sheet.  Plus sheet: excluded gaps occur exactly at the crossing
  lags 17, 29, 41, 46 (witnesses verified in S3).

## S5. Coefficient stopping time: sigma(n) = tau(n) on both sheets
  plus N=1000001: sigma = tau for 500000 of 500000 odd n outside C_b; sigma = infinity for 0 of them; orbit-point disagreements 0; max sigma 111 at n = 626331
  plus N=10000001: sigma = tau for 5000000 of 5000000 odd n outside C_b; sigma = infinity for 0 of them; orbit-point disagreements 0; max sigma 155 at n = 8088063
  plus cycle elements n:(sigma, tau) over 70 steps: 1:(None,1)
  minus N=1000001: sigma = tau for 499991 of 499991 odd n outside C_b; sigma = infinity for 0 of them; orbit-point disagreements 0; max sigma 125 at n = 482817
  minus N=10000001: sigma = tau for 4999991 of 4999991 odd n outside C_b; sigma = infinity for 0 of them; orbit-point disagreements 0; max sigma 172 at n = 5960769
  minus cycle elements n:(sigma, tau) over 70 steps: 1:(None,None) 5:(None,None) 7:(1,1) 17:(None,None) 25:(6,6) 37:(5,5) 41:(3,3) 55:(1,1) 61:(2,2) 91:(1,1)
  PROVED (computer-assisted): a failure of sigma = tau at n is a gate crossing from the start at lag
  l = tau(n) (plus: the first decay prefix does not descend) or l = sigma(n) (minus: a growth prefix
  descends), hence n < G_b(l); the 10^7 census has none, so sigma = tau holds for every odd n >= 3
  with tau(n) <= 38 on the plus sheet and for every odd n outside C_- with sigma(n) <= 35 on the minus
  sheet.  The minus cycles are compatible with the law because their words never become decay words
  (tau = sigma = infinity at every cycle element); a plus cycle would violate it at its minimum
  (tau finite because 2^K > 3^L, sigma infinite).
  plus CST margin: windows first-decay window (0, tau(n)) with mu = n/gate - 1 < 1, n <= 10^7: ['n=63 l=34 K=54 x_l=61 mu=0.7472']; complete for l <= 33 (2 G_b(l) <= 10^7)
  minus CST margin: windows growth prefix (0, l), l < sigma(n) with mu = n/gate - 1 < 1, n <= 10^7: none; complete for l <= 35 (2 G_b(l) <= 10^7)

